Certainly! To create a gRPC status proto mapping using the `google-protobuf` library, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have the `google-protobuf` library installed. You can install it using pip:

```sh
pip install protobuf
```

2. Next, define the gRPC status proto mapping in a `.proto` file. Here's an example of a `status.proto` file:

```proto
syntax = "proto3";

package grpc;

// gRPC status codes
enum StatusCode {
  OK = 0;
  CANCELLED = 1;
  UNKNOWN = 2;
  INVALID_ARGUMENT = 3;
  DEADLINE_EXCEEDED = 4;
  NOT_FOUND = 5;
  ALREADY_EXISTS = 6;
  PERMISSION_DENIED = 7;
  RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED = 8;
  FAILED_PRECONDITION = 9;
  ABORTED = 10;
  OUT_OF_RANGE = 11;
  UNIMPLEMENTED = 12;
  INTERNAL = 13;
  UNAVAILABLE = 14;
  DATA_LOSS = 15;
  UNAUTHENTICATED = 16;
}

// gRPC status message
message Status {
  StatusCode code = 1;
  string message = 2;
  map<string, string> details = 3;
}
```

3. Now, you can generate the Python code using the `protoc` compiler and the `google-protobuf` plugin:

```sh
protoc -I . --python_out=. status.proto
```

This will generate a `status_pb2.py` file containing the Python code for the gRPC status proto mapping.

4. Finally, you can use the generated Python code in your Python project to work with the gRPC status proto mapping. Here's an example of how to use it:

```python
from status_pb2 import StatusCode, Status

# Create a gRPC status message
status = Status()
status.code = StatusCode.OK
status.message = "Operation successful"

# Access the status code and message
print("Status code:", status.code)
print("Status message:", status.message)

# Add details to the status message
status.details["user"] = "john_doe"
status.details["timestamp"] = "2023-06-19T12:00:00Z"

# Access the details
print("User:", status.details.get("user", "N/A"))
print("Timestamp:", status.details.get("timestamp", "N/A"))
```

